The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
```c
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `emit_udiv()` is called with `rn` set to `ARM_R0` (r_scratch) and `rm` set to another register. When `rm` is loaded into `ARM_R0`, it overwrites the value of `rn`, causing both the dividend and divisor in the `jit_udiv()` function to be the same, leading to incorrect division results.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
Reason:
The fix involves loading `rn` into `ARM_R1` before loading `rm` into `ARM_R0`. This ensures that the original value of `rn` is preserved and not overwritten, allowing `jit_udiv()` to correctly use distinct values for the dividend and divisor, thus preventing incorrect division results.

